IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- g7-interpretation-laws: definitions COPIED BY HAND from two other encodings, so that g7-tests-interpretation-laws can test, from the text of those two Laws, two of the readings this group takes at its forks:
-- F7.11 (how the day named as the day of commencement is counted) and F7.14 (whether an Order under Article 2(2) can make consequential amendments of other provisions of the Law).
--
-- Where the copies come from.
-- The Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954 and the Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021 are each encoded in a subject of the commons of their own, je/interpretation-1954 and je/legislation-2021, in the row legalese-2026-10.
-- Every definition below is copied from a module of one of those two rows, at the commit of this branch, and the comment above each copy names the subject, the module and the Article.
-- The copies are textually identical to their sources (same names, same bodies), so that a diff of a copied block against the same block of its source shows drift; comment lines and @ref lines are left out of that comparison.
--
-- What is new here, and what differs from the sources.
-- This header and the "COPY of" comment above each copy are new.  A comment of the source that stood with the copied code is kept; where it says "the brief", "NOTES.md" or "the row", it means those of the SOURCE subject, not of this one.
-- Two things differ from the sources, and nothing else does:
--   * the @ref of a copied function.  The source's carries a locator into the source's own text file, which check.sh would test against the text of the Charities Law and fail.
--     It is replaced by a plain-text citation of the Law, the Article, the subject, the file and the lines.  A noun has no @ref.
--   * the comment lines that quote the Laws' text with a line number are not carried, for the same reason.
--
-- These copies are ASSUMED, NOT RULED, and a hack.
-- L4 cannot import across folders yet (relative imports have not reached main), and check.sh lets a group module import only prelude, daydate, domain and modules of its own group (g7-), so the definitions are copied here.
-- When relative imports reach main, delete this module and let the tests import the two rows.
-- When either row changes, these copies must be re-synced: compare each copied block with the same block of its source module, and copy the changed source over it again.  Do not edit a copied block here.

§ `Copies from the Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954: Article 1(5) and 1(6)`

-- The day on which an enactment expressed to come into operation on a particular day comes into operation, under the text of the Law in force from 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021.

-- COPY of the noun `Basis of a reading` from je/interpretation-1954 (row legalese-2026-10), module je54-nouns.l4, Article 1 (how sure an answer is).  No change from the source.
-- How sure the encoding is of an answer.  The four provisions that the endnotes of the 2019 consolidation date to the Interpretation (Amendment) (Jersey) Law 2003 are answered for the days
-- from 28 March 2003 to 31 December 2018 by inference only.  The third marks a day found under the text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021 that falls on or after 28 September 2021: from then the commencement of an
-- enactment is governed by the Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021, which this row does not encode; that Law gives the same day for a named day (checked by the integrator on 2026-10-28 against Article 3(2), subject je/legislation-2021, source/l_8_2021.current.txt line 48).
DECLARE `Basis of a reading` IS ONE OF
    `read from the deposited text in force on that day`
    `inferred from the endnotes of the 2019 consolidation, not verified against L.16/2003`
    `governed by the 2021 Law, Article 3(2)`

-- COPY of the noun `How an enactment expresses the time of its commencement` from je/interpretation-1954 (row legalese-2026-10), module je54-nouns.l4, Articles 1(4) to 1(7).  No change from the source.
-- Articles 1(4) to 1(7): how an enactment states the time at which it is to come into operation.
DECLARE `How an enactment expresses the time of its commencement` IS ONE OF
    `on a particular day`
    `forthwith`
    `silent as to the time at which it is to come into operation`
    `calculated by reference to its promulgation`

-- COPY of the noun `Commencement as stated` from je/interpretation-1954 (row legalese-2026-10), module je54-nouns.l4, Articles 1(4) to 1(7).  No change from the source.
-- An enactment's commencement as the enactment states it, and the days the question needs.
DECLARE `Commencement as stated` HAS
    `how it is expressed` IS A `How an enactment expresses the time of its commencement` @desc How the enactment states the time at which it is to come into operation
    `the day it names` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc The particular day on which the enactment says it comes into force; NOTHING if it names none
    `the day it was passed or made` IS A DATE @desc The day the enactment was passed or made (for a Law in the text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021, the day it was deemed passed under Article 1(2))
    `the day the question is asked` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc The day on which you are asking; read only if you choose the reading that the text in force on that day governs; NOTHING otherwise

-- COPY of the noun `Day with its basis` from je/interpretation-1954 (row legalese-2026-10), module je54-nouns.l4, Article 1 (answers that carry how sure they are).  No change from the source.
-- Answers that carry how sure they are.
DECLARE `Day with its basis` HAS
    `the day` IS A DATE @desc The day
    `the basis` IS A `Basis of a reading` @desc Whether the answer is read from a deposited text or inferred from the endnotes

-- COPY of the function `Article 1(5): the enactment is expressed to come into operation on a particular day but names none` from je/interpretation-1954 (row legalese-2026-10), module je54-art01-commencement.l4, Article 1(5).  The only change from the source is the @ref: the locator is replaced by a plain-text citation.
-- Refusal (Article 1(5)): an enactment said to come into operation on a particular day, but naming none.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 1(5), text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021 (subject je/interpretation-1954, file source/l_17_1954_20210927.txt, line 9)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 1(5): the enactment is expressed to come into operation on a particular day but names none` MEANS
    REFUSE "the enactment is said to come into operation on a particular day, but no day is named"

-- COPY of the function `Article 1(5) and 1(6): a time calculated from promulgation is not given a day by these Articles` from je/interpretation-1954 (row legalese-2026-10), module je54-art01-commencement.l4, Article 1(7).  The only change from the source is the @ref: the locator is replaced by a plain-text citation.
-- Refusal (Article 1(5), 1(6) and 1(7)): a time calculated from promulgation is not given a day by these Articles, and no period-counting rule exists.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 1(7), text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021 (subject je/interpretation-1954, file source/l_17_1954_20210927.txt, line 11)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`Article 1(5) and 1(6): a time calculated from promulgation is not given a day by these Articles` MEANS
    REFUSE "the time is calculated by reference to promulgation: Article 1(7) fixes the date of promulgation when it is unknown, but neither Law says how a period is counted from it, so no day is given"

-- COPY of the function `Article 1(5) and 1(6): the day under the text` from je/interpretation-1954 (row legalese-2026-10), module je54-art01-commencement.l4, Article 1(5) and 1(6).  The only change from the source is the @ref: the locator is replaced by a plain-text citation.
-- The day on which the enactment is construed as coming into operation (it comes into operation at the start of that day: 1(5), "immediately on the expiration of the previous day").
-- Under 1(5): an enactment expressed to come into operation on a particular day.  Under 1(6): one expressed to come into operation forthwith, or silent, is construed as expressed to come into operation on
-- the day on which it was passed or made, and then 1(5) applies to it (FINDING F1).  An enactment whose time is calculated by reference to its promulgation is not given a day by these Articles.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 1(5) and 1(6), text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021 (subject je/interpretation-1954, file source/l_17_1954_20210927.txt, lines 9 and 10)
GIVEN c IS A `Commencement as stated`
      basis IS A `Basis of a reading`
GIVETH A `Day with its basis`
`Article 1(5) and 1(6): the day under the text` c basis MEANS
    CONSIDER c's `how it is expressed`
    WHEN `on a particular day` THEN
        CONSIDER c's `the day it names`
        WHEN JUST named THEN `Day with its basis` WITH `the day` IS named, `the basis` IS basis
        WHEN NOTHING THEN `Article 1(5): the enactment is expressed to come into operation on a particular day but names none`
    WHEN `forthwith` THEN `Day with its basis` WITH `the day` IS c's `the day it was passed or made`, `the basis` IS basis
    WHEN `silent as to the time at which it is to come into operation` THEN `Day with its basis` WITH `the day` IS c's `the day it was passed or made`, `the basis` IS basis
    WHEN `calculated by reference to its promulgation` THEN `Article 1(5) and 1(6): a time calculated from promulgation is not given a day by these Articles`

§ `Copies from the Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021: Article 3`

-- The moment legislation expressed to come into force on a particular day does so (Article 3(2)), and whether Article 3 governs legislation that has already commenced (Article 3(6)).

-- COPY of the noun `Time of day` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-nouns.l4, Article 3.  No change from the source.
-- A time of day on a day, in whole minutes.
-- The Law never states a time zone; none is carried here, and a caller who has a time must give it in the time kept in Jersey on that day.
DECLARE `Time of day` HAS
    `hour of the day` IS A NUMBER @desc The hour, 0 to 23, on a 24-hour clock
    `minute of the hour` IS A NUMBER @desc The minute, 0 to 59

-- COPY of the noun `Reading L2 of a stated time of day` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-nouns.l4, Article 3(2).  No change from the source.
-- L2: Article 3(2) says a provision expressed to come into force on a particular day is treated as coming into force at the beginning of that day,
-- "unless provision is made to the contrary".
-- Whether a stated time of day is such a provision, the Law does not say.
DECLARE `Reading L2 of a stated time of day` IS ONE OF
    `a stated time of day is provision to the contrary, and the legislation comes into force at that time`
    `a stated time of day does not displace the beginning of the day`

-- COPY of the noun `Reading L3 of the time at which the Law came into force` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-nouns.l4, Article 3(6).  No change from the source.
-- L3: the time at which the Law came into force on 28 September 2021.
-- Articles 3(6) and 11(4) speak of the commencement of Part 2 and Articles 13(1) and 16(1)(a) of the commencement of Part 3; the endnotes record one commencement day for the whole Law.
-- The endnotes give the day, and R&O.112/2021 itself, which could state a time, is not among the sources.
-- Article 3(2) would put it at the beginning of the day.
DECLARE `Reading L3 of the time at which the Law came into force` IS ONE OF
    `the beginning of 28 September 2021, by Article 3(2)`
    `the sources do not state it, so a question that turns on a time of day on 28 September 2021 is refused`

-- COPY of the noun `Moment of coming into force` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-nouns.l4, Article 3.  No change from the source.
-- When a provision comes into force, as far as Article 3 says it.
-- The moment is a day and what the Law says about the time of day on it.
DECLARE `Moment of coming into force` IS ONE OF
    `from the beginning of the day` HAS day IS A DATE
    `from a stated time on the day` HAS day IS A DATE, time IS A `Time of day`
    `from the time it was made on the day` HAS day IS A DATE, `time of making` IS A MAYBE `Time of day`
    `at a time on the day that the Law does not state` HAS day IS A DATE

-- COPY of the function `Article 3(2): the moment legislation expressed to come into force on a particular day does so` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-art03-commencement.l4, Article 3(2).  The only change from the source is the @ref: the locator is replaced by a plain-text citation.
-- Needs: `Time of day`, `Moment of coming into force`, `Reading L2 of a stated time of day`.
-- Answers: when does legislation that is expressed to come into force on a particular day come into force?
-- At the beginning of that day, "unless provision is made to the contrary".
-- A stated time of day is the one thing the Law does not say is such a provision: fork L2, default that it is.
@ref Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021, Article 3(2) (subject je/legislation-2021, file source/l_8_2021.current.txt, line 48)
GIVEN `the day the legislation names` IS A DATE
      `the time of day the legislation states for it, if any` IS A MAYBE `Time of day`
      reading IS A `Reading L2 of a stated time of day`
GIVETH A `Moment of coming into force`
`Article 3(2): the moment legislation expressed to come into force on a particular day does so` `the day the legislation names` `the time of day the legislation states for it, if any` reading MEANS
    CONSIDER `the time of day the legislation states for it, if any`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `from the beginning of the day` `the day the legislation names`
    WHEN JUST stated
        THEN CONSIDER reading
            WHEN `a stated time of day is provision to the contrary, and the legislation comes into force at that time`
                THEN `from a stated time on the day` `the day the legislation names` stated
            WHEN `a stated time of day does not displace the beginning of the day`
                THEN `from the beginning of the day` `the day the legislation names`

-- COPY of the function `Article 3(6): Article 3 governs when the legislation commenced` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-art03-commencement.l4, Article 3(6).  The only change from the source is the @ref: the locator is replaced by a plain-text citation.
-- Needs: `Reading L3 of the time at which the Law came into force`.
-- Answers: does Article 3 govern when this legislation commenced?
-- It does not for legislation that "had commenced before the commencement of this Part", and Part 2 came into force on 28 September 2021 (endnotes, Table of Legislation History).
-- The caller gives the day on which the legislation (or provision) commenced, under whatever rule applied, or nothing if it has not commenced.
-- Not commenced: Article 3 governs.
-- Commenced on or before 27 September 2021: it commenced before Part 2 did, so Article 3 does not affect it.
-- Commenced after 28 September 2021: Article 3 governs.
-- Commenced ON 28 September 2021: whether that is "before" depends on the time of day at which Part 2 came into force that day, fork L3.
@ref Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021, Article 3(6) and the endnotes' Table of Legislation History (subject je/legislation-2021, file source/l_8_2021.current.txt, line 54 and lines 258 to 261)
GIVEN `the day on which the legislation commenced, if it has` IS A MAYBE DATE
      reading IS A `Reading L3 of the time at which the Law came into force`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 3(6): Article 3 governs when the legislation commenced` `the day on which the legislation commenced, if it has` reading MEANS
    CONSIDER `the day on which the legislation commenced, if it has`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE
    WHEN JUST commenced
        THEN
            BRANCH
                IF commenced LESS THAN YMD 2021 9 28 THEN FALSE
                IF commenced GREATER THAN YMD 2021 9 28 THEN TRUE
                OTHERWISE
                    CONSIDER reading
                    WHEN `the beginning of 28 September 2021, by Article 3(2)` THEN TRUE
                    WHEN `the sources do not state it, so a question that turns on a time of day on 28 September 2021 is refused`
                        THEN REFUSE "the sources do not say at what time of day on 28 September 2021 Part 2 of the Law came into force, so whether legislation that commenced that day commenced before it cannot be answered"

§ `Copies from the Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021: Articles 2, 9(5) and 9(6)`

-- What the power to make incidental, supplemental or consequential provision does for an instrument that amends other legislation (Article 9(5), limited by Article 9(6)).

-- COPY of the noun `Form of legislation` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-nouns.l4, Articles 1, 2, 7, 9 and 11.  No change from the source.
-- The forms the Law names: Laws (Article 1 "Law"), Regulations including Triennial Regulations, Acts of the States, Orders, other instruments
-- (Article 1 "Jersey subordinate legislation"), and UK extended legislation (Article 1).
DECLARE `Form of legislation` IS ONE OF
    `a Law`
    `Regulations`
    `Triennial Regulations`
    `an Act of the States that is Jersey legislation`
    `an Order`
    `another instrument having legislative effect in Jersey`
    `UK extended legislation`

-- COPY of the noun `Reading L5 of legislation made by the States` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-nouns.l4, Article 9(6)(b).  No change from the source.
-- L5: Articles 7(2), 9(6)(b) and 11(3) speak of legislation "not made by the States" and the Law does not say what that is.
DECLARE `Reading L5 of legislation made by the States` IS ONE OF
    `made by the States means adopted by the States Assembly, as Article 2(1)(a) and 2(3)(a) put it, so it covers Laws, Regulations and Acts`
    `made by the States means a Law only`

-- COPY of the noun `Reading L6 of the limits in Article 9(6)` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-nouns.l4, Article 9(6).  No change from the source.
-- L6: Article 9(6) says the incidental power of Article 9(5) "is limited ... to amending" certain legislation.
DECLARE `Reading L6 of the limits in Article 9(6)` IS ONE OF
    `the limits bear on provision that amends other legislation, and incidental provision of any other kind is not limited by them`
    `the power in Article 9(5) is limited to amendments of the permitted kind, and authorises incidental provision of no other kind`

-- COPY of the noun `Legislation to be amended` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-nouns.l4, Article 9(5) and 9(6).  No change from the source.
-- The legislation that an incidental provision under Article 9(5) would amend.
DECLARE `Legislation to be amended` HAS
    `its form` IS A `Form of legislation` @desc The form of the legislation that would be amended
    `it is the Human Rights (Jersey) Law 2000` IS A BOOLEAN @desc TRUE if the legislation to be amended is the Human Rights (Jersey) Law 2000
    `it is the legislation giving the power to make the instrument` IS A BOOLEAN @desc TRUE if the legislation to be amended is the legislation that gives the power to make the instrument doing the amending

-- COPY of the noun `What the incidental power does` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-nouns.l4, Article 9(5) and 9(6).  No change from the source.
-- What the incidental power of Article 9(5), limited by Article 9(6), does for a provision.
DECLARE `What the incidental power does` IS ONE OF
    `Article 9(5) authorises the provision`
    `Article 9(6) bars the provision`
    `Article 9(5) does not apply because provision is made to the contrary`

-- COPY of the function `Article 2: the legislation was made by the States` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-art02-making.l4, Article 2(1)(a) and 2(3)(a), as Article 9(6)(b) needs them.  The only change from the source is the @ref: the locator is replaced by a plain-text citation.
-- Articles 7(2), 9(6)(b) and 11(3) speak of legislation "not made by the States", and the Law does not say what that is: fork L5.
-- On the reading taken, the legislation the States makes is what Article 2 says is made by the States Assembly's adoption:
-- a Law (2(1)(a)), and Regulations, Triennial Regulations and Acts (2(3)(a)).
-- Orders and other instruments are signed (2(3)(b)); UK extended legislation is not made in Jersey at all.
@ref Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021, Article 2(1)(a) and 2(3)(a) (subject je/legislation-2021, file source/l_8_2021.current.txt, lines 33 and 38)
GIVEN form IS A `Form of legislation`
      reading IS A `Reading L5 of legislation made by the States`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Article 2: the legislation was made by the States` form reading MEANS
    CONSIDER reading
    WHEN `made by the States means adopted by the States Assembly, as Article 2(1)(a) and 2(3)(a) put it, so it covers Laws, Regulations and Acts`
        THEN CONSIDER form
            WHEN `a Law` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `Regulations` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `Triennial Regulations` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `an Act of the States that is Jersey legislation` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `an Order` THEN FALSE
            WHEN `another instrument having legislative effect in Jersey` THEN FALSE
            WHEN `UK extended legislation` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `made by the States means a Law only`
        THEN CONSIDER form
            WHEN `a Law` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

-- COPY of the function `the limit in Article 9(6)(a) is met` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-art09-10-subordinate-powers.l4, Article 9(6)(a).  The only change from the source is the @ref: the locator is replaced by a plain-text citation.
-- 9(6)(a): met unless the instrument is Regulations and the legislation to be amended is the Human Rights (Jersey) Law 2000 or the legislation giving the power.
@ref Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021, Article 9(6)(a) (subject je/legislation-2021, file source/l_8_2021.current.txt, line 99)
GIVEN `the form of the instrument being made` IS A `Form of legislation`
      `the legislation to be amended` IS A `Legislation to be amended`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the limit in Article 9(6)(a) is met` `the form of the instrument being made` `the legislation to be amended` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the form of the instrument being made`
    WHEN `Regulations`
        THEN     NOT `the legislation to be amended`'s `it is the Human Rights (Jersey) Law 2000`
             AND NOT `the legislation to be amended`'s `it is the legislation giving the power to make the instrument`
    WHEN `Triennial Regulations`
        THEN     NOT `the legislation to be amended`'s `it is the Human Rights (Jersey) Law 2000`
             AND NOT `the legislation to be amended`'s `it is the legislation giving the power to make the instrument`
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- COPY of the function `the limit in Article 9(6)(b) is met` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-art09-10-subordinate-powers.l4, Article 9(6)(b).  The only change from the source is the @ref: the locator is replaced by a plain-text citation.
-- 9(6)(b): met unless the instrument was not made by the States and the legislation to be amended was made by the States.
@ref Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021, Article 9(6)(b) (subject je/legislation-2021, file source/l_8_2021.current.txt, line 100)
GIVEN `the form of the instrument being made` IS A `Form of legislation`
      `the legislation to be amended` IS A `Legislation to be amended`
      `the reading of legislation made by the States` IS A `Reading L5 of legislation made by the States`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the limit in Article 9(6)(b) is met` `the form of the instrument being made` `the legislation to be amended` `the reading of legislation made by the States` MEANS
    IF `Article 2: the legislation was made by the States` `the form of the instrument being made` `the reading of legislation made by the States`
    THEN TRUE
    ELSE NOT `Article 2: the legislation was made by the States` (`the legislation to be amended`'s `its form`) `the reading of legislation made by the States`

-- COPY of the function `Article 9(5) and (6): what the incidental power does for the provision` from je/legislation-2021 (row legalese-2026-10), module je21-art09-10-subordinate-powers.l4, Article 9(5) and 9(6).  The only change from the source is the @ref: the locator is replaced by a plain-text citation.
-- Answers: does the incidental power of Article 9(5), limited by Article 9(6), authorise this provision?
--   `Article 9(5) authorises the provision`:                  the incidental power covers it.
--   `Article 9(6) bars the provision`:                        the limit in 9(6)(a) or (b) rules it out.
--   `Article 9(5) does not apply because provision is made to the contrary`: the Law cannot say what applies instead.
-- 9(6)(a), for Regulations (and Triennial Regulations): the power is limited to amending legislation other than the Human Rights (Jersey) Law 2000 or the legislation giving the power to make the Regulations.
-- 9(6)(b), for legislation not made by the States: the power is limited to amending other legislation not made by the States.
-- So an Order of a Minister, which is signed and not adopted (Article 2(3)(b)), can amend an Order and cannot amend a Law or Regulations.
-- Fork L5 gives "made by the States"; fork L6 says whether the limits bear on provision that is not an amendment.
-- 9(6) names Regulations and legislation not made by the States only.
-- An Act of the States is neither, so on the text neither limit bears on it; NOTES.md section 10 asks a domain expert whether that is intended.
@ref Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021, Article 9(5) and (6) (subject je/legislation-2021, file source/l_8_2021.current.txt, lines 97 to 100)
GIVEN `the form of the instrument being made` IS A `Form of legislation`
      `the legislation to be amended` IS A `Legislation to be amended`
      `the provision is an amendment of other legislation` IS A BOOLEAN
      `provision is made to the contrary` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the reading of legislation made by the States` IS A `Reading L5 of legislation made by the States`
      `the reading of the limits` IS A `Reading L6 of the limits in Article 9(6)`
GIVETH A `What the incidental power does`
`Article 9(5) and (6): what the incidental power does for the provision` `the form of the instrument being made` `the legislation to be amended` `the provision is an amendment of other legislation` `provision is made to the contrary` `the reading of legislation made by the States` `the reading of the limits` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `provision is made to the contrary`
            THEN `Article 9(5) does not apply because provision is made to the contrary`
        IF NOT `the provision is an amendment of other legislation`
            THEN CONSIDER `the reading of the limits`
                 WHEN `the limits bear on provision that amends other legislation, and incidental provision of any other kind is not limited by them`
                     THEN `Article 9(5) authorises the provision`
                 WHEN `the power in Article 9(5) is limited to amendments of the permitted kind, and authorises incidental provision of no other kind`
                     THEN `Article 9(6) bars the provision`
        OTHERWISE
            IF     `the limit in Article 9(6)(a) is met` `the form of the instrument being made` `the legislation to be amended`
               AND `the limit in Article 9(6)(b) is met` `the form of the instrument being made` `the legislation to be amended` `the reading of legislation made by the States`
            THEN `Article 9(5) authorises the provision`
            ELSE `Article 9(6) bars the provision`
